Automatic Headlight Control using STM32 (LDR + LED)
📌 Overview

This project implements an Automatic Headlight Control System using an STM32 microcontroller and an LDR (Light Dependent Resistor). The system detects ambient light intensity and automatically switches an LED (representing a vehicle headlight) ON or OFF based on a predefined threshold value.

🎯 Features
Real-time ambient light detection
ADC-based LDR signal processing
Automatic ON/OFF LED control
Simple and efficient embedded system
Low-cost implementation
🧠 Working Principle

The LDR produces an analog voltage depending on light intensity. This signal is read by the STM32 using its ADC. If the environment is dark (low ADC value), the LED is turned ON. If the environment is bright (high ADC value), the LED is turned OFF.

🔧 Hardware Used
STM32 (e.g., STM32F4 series / Trainer Kit)
LDR (Light Dependent Resistor)
LED (Headlight simulation)
Resistor (for voltage divider if required)
Connecting wires
🔌 Pin Configuration
Component	Pin
LDR (ADC Input)	PA0
LED Output	PB3
⚙️ Software Configuration
IDE: STM32CubeIDE
Language: C (HAL Drivers)
ADC Resolution: 12-bit (0–4095)
Threshold Value: 2000
📊 Output Behavior
Light Condition	ADC Value	LED State
Dark	< 2000	ON
Bright	≥ 2000	OFF
🚀 How It Works
STM32 reads analog value from LDR using ADC
The ADC value is compared with threshold (2000)
If value < 2000 → Dark → LED ON
If value ≥ 2000 → Bright → LED OFF
System updates continuously every 100 ms
▶️ How to Run
Open project in STM32CubeIDE
Build the project
Connect STM32 via ST-Link/USB
Flash the code
Vary light intensity on LDR
Observe LED switching automatically
⚠️ Notes
Adjust threshold value (2000) depending on lighting conditions
Ensure proper LDR voltage divider circuit
Avoid noisy ADC readings (add filtering if needed)
Keep LDR exposed to ambient light for accurate sensing
